Usage is straightforward — a parent screen owns the cart state and renders:

```tsx
<CheckoutScreen
  cartItems={cartItems}
  onBack={() => navigate('/cart')}
  onPay={async (payload) => {
    await submitOrder(payload); // your API call / Telegram invoice flow
  }}
/>
```

Notes on a few decisions:
- Uses `var(--tg-theme-*)` custom properties with fallback colors so it matches whatever Telegram theme (light/dark) is active, while still looking correct standalone in a browser.
- Validates required address fields on blur and on submit; the Pay button is disabled while submitting or if the cart is empty, and shows a spinner during the async `onPay` call.
- Hooks into the native Telegram `BackButton` when `window.Telegram.WebApp` exists, and cleans up the listener on unmount; the in-header back arrow covers testing outside Telegram.
- Free delivery over a threshold is just a simple example rule — swap `FREE_DELIVERY_THRESHOLD`/`DELIVERY_FEE` for your real pricing logic.
